    "original_title": "Webiga Shabeelle oo Ku Dhowaaday Heerka Digniinta Fatahaadaha",
    "slug": "shabelle-river-water-levels-near-flood-warning-threshold-in-jowhar",
    "summary": "Water levels in the Shabelle River at Jowhar have risen significantly over the past week, approaching flood warning levels, according to FAO-SWALIM. The river gauge increased from 2.48 to 4.70 meters, and forecasts predict moderate to heavy rains in parts of the region.",
    "content": "Water levels in the Shabelle River near Jowhar have risen sharply over the past week, moving close to the flood warning threshold, according to the latest data from FAO-SWALIM. The river gauge at Jowhar increased from 2.48 meters to 4.70 meters, a rise of 2.22 meters.\n\nThe current level is just 30 centimeters below the moderate risk threshold of 5 meters, while the high-risk threshold stands at 5.25 meters. FAO-SWALIM reported that the rate of increase has slowed, but moderate to heavy rains are expected in parts of the region, which could further elevate water levels.\n\nThe Shabelle River is one of Somalia's two major rivers and is prone to seasonal flooding, particularly during the Gu (April-June) and Deyr (October-December) rainy seasons. In recent years, recurrent droughts and floods have compounded humanitarian challenges in the region. Jowhar, located in the Middle Shabelle region, is a key agricultural hub and has experienced severe flooding in the past, notably in 2020 and 2021.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, these developments are a cause for concern, as many have family and investments in the affected areas. Flooding can displace communities, disrupt livelihoods, and exacerbate food insecurity, often prompting diaspora-led relief efforts. The diaspora plays a vital role in supporting relatives and funding local recovery initiatives.\n\nMonitoring continues as authorities and humanitarian agencies assess the situation. The FAO-SWALIM data serves as a critical early warning tool for potential flooding, enabling preparedness and mitigation measures. The diaspora is encouraged to stay informed and coordinate with local networks to provide timely assistance if needed.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/10/11/webiga-shabeelle-oo-ku-dhowaaday-heerka-digniinta-fatahaadaha/",
