    "content": "At least 12 people were killed and 309 others wounded in an attack on Riyadh airport in Saudi Arabia on Saturday, according to reports. The assault was carried out by the Houthi group based in Yemen, marking a significant escalation in the conflict between the two sides.\n\nThe Houthi movement has been engaged in a protracted conflict with a Saudi-led coalition since 2015. This attack represents one of the more severe incidents targeting Saudi infrastructure, though details about the specific methods used remain limited. The airport is a major hub in the Saudi capital, and the casualty figures underscore the scale of the violence.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, many of whom reside in Saudi Arabia and the wider Gulf region, this attack raises immediate concerns about safety and security. Saudi Arabia hosts a large Somali expatriate community, and any instability there can directly affect their livelihoods and well-being. Additionally, the broader Yemen conflict has already displaced millions and disrupted regional trade routes that are vital to Somalia's economy.\n\nInternational reactions have been muted so far, but the incident is likely to heighten tensions in the region. The conflict in Yemen has drawn widespread criticism due to its humanitarian toll, and this attack may prompt renewed calls for de-escalation. The Somali diaspora, with its deep connections to both Yemen and Saudi Arabia, watches such developments closely.\n\nAs the situation develops, accurate information remains crucial. The Somali community is advised to stay informed through reliable sources and to follow any security advisories issued by local authorities.\n\nRead the original report from Horseed Media here: https://horseedmedia.net/xuutiyiinta-yemen-oo-weerar-dhimasho-dhaawac-sababay-ku-qaaday-garoonka-diyaaradaha-riyaad/426389/",
