    "id": "news-1791665609241-0",
    "title": "Somali Government Loses Three Towns in 48 Hours as Al-Shabaab and Jubaland Advance",
    "original_title": "Somalia&#8217;s govt loses three towns in 48 hours as rivals advance",
    "slug": "somali-government-loses-three-towns-in-48-hours-as-al-shabaab-and-jubaland-advance",
    "summary": "Al-Shabaab militants and Jubaland forces have captured three towns from Somali government control in two days, severing a key supply route and intensifying pressure on Mogadishu.",
    "content": "MOGADISHU, Somalia — In a span of 48 hours, the Somali federal government has lost control of three towns to two separate armed groups, according to local officials and security sources. Fighters from the militant group Al-Shabaab seized the town of Hees in the central Hiran region on Friday following a brief clash with Somali National Army troops. Hees lies near the strategic town of Mataban, positioned along a critical road that links Mataban to the regional capital, Beledweyne.\n\nThe capture of Hees effectively cuts a vital supply line for government forces operating in the area, military analysts say. The road had served as a key conduit for reinforcements and logistics to federal troops stationed in Hiran. Meanwhile, in the south, forces loyal to the semi-autonomous Jubaland administration took control of a town previously held by federal authorities, according to multiple reports. The name of the southern town was not immediately confirmed by federal officials.\n\nThe losses highlight the ongoing challenge facing the Somali government as it battles Al-Shabaab while managing tensions with federal member states. Al-Shabaab has intensified its attacks in recent months, targeting military and civilian infrastructure. The group often launches coordinated assaults on isolated army bases, exploiting gaps in government control. Jubaland, meanwhile, has had a strained relationship with Mogadishu over political and security issues, including the recent electoral dispute in the region.\n\nThe Somali diaspora, which maintains close ties to family and business interests in these regions, is likely to feel the impact. Many diaspora members have invested in local businesses and properties in areas now under threat, and remittances flow through informal networks that can be disrupted by conflict. Additionally, the loss of territory may further delay plans for national elections and security sector reforms, issues that diaspora communities closely follow.\n\nThe federal government has not yet issued a detailed response to the territorial losses. Security experts warn that without regaining control of the supply route and addressing the rift with Jubaland, government forces could face further setbacks. The African Union Transition Mission in Somalia (ATMIS) continues to draw down its troops, raising concerns about the ability of Somali forces to hold territory.\n\nAs the situation develops, the government in Mogadishu faces mounting pressure to respond effectively. The coming days will be critical in determining whether these losses are temporary or signal a broader deterioration in the government's position.\n\nRead the original report from Somalia Guardian here: https://www.somaliguardian.com/news/somalia-news/somalia-government-loses-three-towns-48-hours/",
    "source": "somaliguardian.com",
    "source_name": "Somalia Guardian",
    "original_url": "https://www.somaliguardian.com/news/somalia-news/somalia-government-loses-three-towns-48-hours/",
    "country": "Somalia",
    "category": "Security",
    "published_at": "2026-10-10T18:23:34.000Z",
