    "id": "news-1791650233480-7",
    "title": "Somali Literary Icon Dr. Maxamed Daahir Afrax Dies in London at 69",
    "original_title": "Maxamed Daahir Afrax: Xusuus Toolmoon iyo Raad aan Tirmayn",
    "slug": "somali-literary-icon-dr-maxamed-daahir-afrax-dies-in-london-at-69",
    "summary": "Dr. Maxamed Daahir Afrax, a prominent Somali writer and intellectual, passed away in London on October 9, 2021. Born in 1952 in Jarriiban, Mudug region, he left a lasting legacy through his writings in Somali, Arabic, and English.",
    "content": "Dr. Maxamed Daahir Afrax, a celebrated Somali author and scholar, died on October 9, 2021, in a hospital in London. He was 69 years old. Born in 1952 in the Jarriiban district of the Mudug region, Dr. Afrax was widely recognized for his literary contributions in Somali, Arabic, and English, with a particular emphasis on Somali.\n\nHe began his education at a Quranic school in his hometown of Jarriiban before moving to Mogadishu for further studies. Over the years, he became a leading figure in Somali literature, known for his poetry, plays, and essays that explored Somali culture, identity, and social issues. His work often reflected the complexities of Somali life and the diaspora experience.\n\nDr. Afrax's passing is a significant loss for the Somali diaspora, particularly for those who grew up with his writings. His literary works served as a bridge between generations, preserving Somali heritage and language for those living abroad. Many in the diaspora remember his contributions to Somali literature and intellectual thought, which continue to inspire young writers and scholars.\n\nIn addition to his literary achievements, Dr. Afrax was an advocate for education and cultural preservation. He believed in the power of language to unite Somalis across the globe. His death has prompted an outpouring of tributes from Somali communities worldwide, with many recalling his impact on their understanding of Somali identity.\n\nDr. Afrax's legacy endures through his extensive body of work, which remains a vital resource for Somali studies. He is survived by his family and a global community of readers and admirers. His contributions to Somali literature and culture will be remembered for generations to come.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/10/10/maxamed-daahir-afrax-xusuus-toolmoon-iyo-raad-aan-tirmayn/",
